    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I keep having trouble with my bike chain\u2014it keeps popping off. Any ideas why that happens?",
          "user_rubrik": "Why does my bike chain keep derailing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "A bike chain can pop off for several reasons. Common causes include improper adjustments, which may stem from a loose derailleur or limit screws that need adjusting. If the chain is not properly aligned with the gears or if there are any bent or broken links, that can also lead to issues. Additionally, if the chain is too loose or worn out, it may not stay on the sprockets as it should. It's important to inspect the chain, derailleurs, and limit screws to ensure everything is functioning correctly. If these parts are in good condition, consider checking the chain tension and replacing any worn components.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0014-28-07476:0": 0.9383647441864014,
            "clueweb22-en0014-28-07476:7": 0.9166640043258667,
            "clueweb22-en0020-44-10600:0": 0.8971329927444458,
            "clueweb22-en0032-02-03041:8": 0.4871545732021332,
            "clueweb22-en0044-64-01594:5": 0.3116212487220764,
            "clueweb22-en0020-44-10600:19": 0.27553507685661316,
            "clueweb22-en0010-76-17069:1": 0.2410339117050171,
            "clueweb22-en0014-28-07476:6": 0.12531225383281708,
            "clueweb22-en0014-28-07476:1": 0.028739750385284424
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I recently moved to a city with more hills and rough paths;",
            "I'm a casual bike rider;",
            "I am not really handy with DIY;"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Could the chain itself be the cause of my chain dropping, even though the derailleur seems properly aligned?",
          "user_rubrik": "Could the chain itself be the cause of my chain dropping, even though the derailleur seems properly aligned?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, the chain itself can definitely be a cause of it dropping, even if the derailleur seems properly aligned. A worn or stretched chain may not engage properly with the teeth of the chainrings, leading to it slipping off. Additionally, if the chain is too loose, it can fall off more easily during shifts. It's important to regularly check the condition of your chain and replace it if it shows signs of wear. If you haven't done so recently, consider inspecting the chain for any damage or excessive wear, as this could be contributing to your issue.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0032-02-03041:7": -0.25715571641921997
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "What\u2019s the right length for a bike chain? Does it depend on the bike?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is the perfect length of a chain for a bike?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "The right length for a bike chain does depend on the specific bike, as it varies based on the number of gears and the configuration of the bike. Generally, a new chain should measure 12 inches across 12 links. If it measures 12 1/16 inches or more, it indicates wear and it's time for a replacement. To determine the correct chain length for your bike, you can count the number of teeth on the largest front chainring and the largest rear cog, and calculate the chainstay distance. Additionally, most new chains are long enough and may require links to be removed for the best fit. It's essential to ensure that the chain is not too long or too short to avoid issues while riding.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0024-98-15927:1": 2.536802053451538,
            "clueweb22-en0020-85-09566:16": 1.9660170078277588,
            "clueweb22-en0024-98-15927:0": 1.9039490222930908,
            "clueweb22-en0006-40-14458:4": 1.6989268064498901,
            "clueweb22-en0033-46-10793:4": 1.4061243534088135,
            "clueweb22-en0019-33-03300:3": 1.3884286880493164,
            "clueweb22-en0009-91-00148:0": 1.3322886228561401,
            "clueweb22-en0024-98-15927:7": 1.3222557306289673,
            "clueweb22-en0002-27-12660:3": 1.2700278759002686,
            "clueweb22-en0016-80-06317:0": 0.9913979768753052,
            "clueweb22-en0010-25-00830:0": 0.9285531640052795,
            "clueweb22-en0020-85-09566:15": 0.8319493532180786,
            "clueweb22-en0019-62-07576:3": 0.6638600826263428,
            "clueweb22-en0006-40-14458:5": 0.48870426416397095,
            "clueweb22-en0039-68-06653:0": 0.4067806899547577,
            "clueweb22-en0024-98-15927:10": 0.1879952996969223,
            "clueweb22-en0024-98-15927:11": 0.15085509419441223,
            "clueweb22-en0028-57-07555:5": 0.03120291233062744,
            "clueweb22-en0007-14-08949:0": 0.025006473064422607
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s still a bit confusing for me\u2014could you walk me through a really simple way to check or adjust to the right chain length, especially for a mountain bike?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is the perfect length of a chain for a bike?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "To check or adjust the chain length on your mountain bike, follow these simple steps:\n\n1. **Remove the Old Chain**: First, take off the old chain from your bike. You can use a chain tool or master link pliers if your chain has a master link.\n\n2. **Identify Chainring and Cog Sizes**: Shift your bike to the largest front chainring and the largest rear cog. This position is important for measuring the chain length accurately.\n\n3. **Wrap the New Chain**: Take your new chain and wrap it around the largest front chainring and the largest rear cog. Ensure that the chain is routed properly through the rear derailleur.\n\n4. **Check Chain Length**: Hold the chain at the 5 o'clock position (where it would be if installed) and see how much chain is left over. You want the chain to be taut but not excessively tight. If the chain is too long, you will need to remove links.\n\n5. **Cut to Size**: If the chain is too long, count the number of links you need to remove. A good rule of thumb is to remove links in pairs. After cutting, reattach the",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0009-91-00148:2": 1.0174407958984375,
            "clueweb22-en0016-80-06317:0": 0.682996392250061,
            "clueweb22-en0010-25-00830:0": 0.6535236835479736,
            "clueweb22-en0009-91-00148:3": 0.21037167310714722
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I sometimes get overwhelmed by too many technical choices;",
            "I'm a casual bike rider;",
            "I am not really handy with DIY;"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s helpful, thanks. If I take my bike into a shop, will they do all this for me, or do I need to know exactly what chain I want before I go?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is the perfect length of a chain for a bike?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "You don't necessarily need to know exactly what chain you want before taking your bike to a shop. Most bike shops can help you identify the right chain for your bike based on its make and model. However, it can be helpful to have some basic information, like the type of bike you have (mountain, road, etc.) and any specific issues you're facing with the chain. This way, the shop can provide you with the best options and advice for your situation.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0010-25-00830:0": 0.9155910611152649,
            "clueweb22-en0016-80-06317:0": 0.9122636914253235
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "4: I sometimes get overwhelmed by too many technical choices;",
            "7: I'm a casual bike rider;"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great, that takes some pressure off! Back to home repairs\u2014if I do get a chain and need to open it, how do I actually use a chain breaker tool?",
          "user_rubrik": "How do I use a chain breaker to open my bike chain?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To use a chain breaker tool, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Position the Chain**: Place the chain in the tool so that the pin you want to push out is aligned with the tool's pin pusher.\n\n2. **Adjust the Tool**: Make sure the chain is securely held in place, and adjust the tool so that the pusher is just touching the pin.\n\n3. **Push the Pin**: Turn the handle or knob of the tool to push the pin out. Go slowly and ensure that the pin is coming out straight. If it feels stuck, stop and check to avoid damaging the chain.\n\n4. **Remove the Chain Link**: Once the pin is pushed out, you can separate the chain links. If you're connecting a new chain, align the links and insert a new pin.\n\n5. **Reassemble**: If you're reattaching the chain, ensure that the new pin is inserted correctly and secure it by pushing it back into place with the tool.\n\nAlways refer to the specific instructions that come with your chain breaker tool, as designs may vary slightly.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0020-78-15858:3": 0.9605611562728882,
            "clueweb22-en0015-74-07553:0": 0.7883821725845337,
            "clueweb22-en0018-14-06351:0": 0.6667323112487793,
            "clueweb22-en0035-88-16391:0": 0.292329877614975,
            "clueweb22-en0043-65-11643:0": 0.2755153477191925,
            "clueweb22-en0013-08-15764:0": 0.2284940481185913,
            "clueweb22-en0008-63-17002:0": 0.2267751842737198,
            "clueweb22-en0005-30-08556:0": 0.11859145015478134,
            "clueweb22-en0031-57-03286:0": 0.11581498384475708
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am not really handy with DIY."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, I think I get it\u2014might take some practice! So, what should I use to lubricate a bike chain?",
          "user_rubrik": "What should I use to lubricate a bike chain?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To lubricate your bike chain, you have several good options depending on your riding conditions. For dry and dusty environments, the Muc Off Dry Lube is a great choice as it uses a wax-based formula that repels contaminants and is biodegradable. If you're looking for a budget-friendly option, the White Lightning Epic Ride Bicycle Chain Lubricant works well in both moist and dry conditions, providing protection for up to 100 miles. For a more versatile lubricant that performs in various conditions, the Park Tool CL-1 Synthetic Blend Bicycle Chain Lube, which contains PTFE, is ideal as it helps reduce noise and extends the life of your drivetrain. Make sure to apply the lubricant to a clean and dry chain for the best results.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0011-31-03597:6": 0.217376708984375
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 8,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s interesting. When should I add grease on a bike chain?",
          "user_rubrik": "When should I add grease on a bike chain? ",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "You should add grease to your bike chain whenever it looks dry or after cleaning it. Regular lubrication is important to maintain optimal performance and protect the drivetrain. For most riders, it's recommended to lubricate the chain at least once a month, but if you ride frequently or in harsh conditions, you may want to do it more often\u2014ideally after every ride if the chain gets particularly dirty. Always clean the chain before applying new grease to ensure it's free of dirt and contaminants.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0007-95-05927:13": 2.2217066287994385,
            "clueweb22-en0024-04-10077:1": 2.166560649871826,
