          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "It's normal for the chain to derail when pedaling backwards on a derailleur bike, as the rear derailleur guides the chain to the side of the front chainring. However, if you're experiencing issues on a single-speed or internally geared bike, it might be due to a loose chain, a bent chainwheel, or a bad chain line. You can try adjusting the chain tension or checking the chain line to resolve the issue. If you're not comfortable with DIY, consider consulting a professional mechanic for assistance. Choose a derailleur that suits your bike type and riding style, and consider upgrading to a clutched rear derailleur for improved chain retention on rough terrain. Remember to also check your sprockets, derailleurs, shifters, and cranksets for wear and tear, as these can also cause derailing issues. Get your bike serviced regularly to prevent such problems. Consider using a chain retention technology from mountain biking to ensure your chain stays in place. You can also",
