      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "So it\u2019s not a standard size then\u2014sounds complicated. Is there a simple way for someone not very handy, like me, to measure and set the right chain length without a ton of tools?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is the perfect length of a chain for a bike?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "The simplest method is to use your old chain as a guide, provided it was correctly sized. First, verify your old chain's length: shift to the smallest front and rear sprockets \u2013 there should be no slack. Then, shift to the largest front and rear sprockets; the chain should have two slight bends at each pulley. If your old chain passes these checks, simply lay your new chain next to it on a flat surface and cut it to the same length. This method generally requires a chain tool for cutting.",
