          "user_rubrik": "What is the perfect length of a chain for a bike?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "There isn\u2019t one \u201cperfect\u201d link count\u2014chain length depends on your biggest chainring and largest rear cog. Use this simple sizing:  - Big\u2013big method: Route the chain around the largest front ring and largest rear cog, bypassing the rear derailleur. Pull it tight, then add one full link (one inner + one outer). Cut there and install the quick link. For full-suspension frames, add one more link.  Quick checks after installing: - Big ring + biggest cog: Rear derailleur cage should be angled forward but not pulled dead-straight. - Small ring + smallest cog: Chain shouldn\u2019t hang slack or let the cage fold under.  Notes: - Works for 1x and 2x/3x drivetrains. - Use a chain that matches your speed (e.g., 10/11/12\u2011speed) and respect directional arrows on some chains/quick links.  If you\u2019re not set up with tools, a shop can size and fit a chain in minutes and confirm everything (limits, B\u2011tension) so it won\u2019t drop.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0017-28-10944:0": -2.203620433807373,
            "clueweb22-en0028-61-00677:0": -2.4807987213134766,
