          "text": "there isn \u2019 t one \u201c perfect \u201d length \u2014 it depends on your bike \u2019 s drivetrain ( single \u2011 speed vs multi \u2011 speed ), the largest chainring / cassette sprocket sizes, and your rear derailleur \u2019 s capacity. a few practical rules : - match the chain type first : pick a chain width rated for your cassette ( e. g., 8 / 9 / 10 / 11 / 12 \u2011 speed ). - most chains are 1 / 2 \" pitch and come long ( often ~ 116 links ). you cut to fit. - easiest sizing ( multi \u2011 speed derailleurs ) : wrap the new chain around the largest chainring and largest rear cog ( bypass the derailleur ), pull it snug, then add 2 full links ( one inner + one outer ). that \u2019 s the standard safe length. - if you still have the old chain and it was correct, lay the new chain next to it and match rivet spacing. - for single \u2011 speed / track bikes you need exact tension ; measure chainstay length and choose link count accordingly. if you tell me bike type, number of speeds, largest chainring and biggest rear cog ( or",
